<% render partial: "about/subnav" %>

<div class="box wide">
  <div class="story_text">
    <p>
      Thneed is a community for thoughtful discussion about how artificial
      intelligence changes human work, creativity, power, and agency. We share
      durable links, ask careful questions, and try to understand the systems
      being built around us.
    </p>

    <h2 id="guidelines">Community guidelines</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Curiosity and evidence:</strong>
      Bring a genuine question, useful experience, or evidence others can
      examine. Distinguish what you know from what you infer. Strong claims
      deserve strong support; changing your mind is welcome.
    </p>

    <p>
      <strong>Respectful disagreement:</strong>
      Address ideas and consequences rather than attacking people. Be specific,
      charitable, and open to correction. Harassment, abuse, bigotry, and
      dehumanizing language are not welcome. Disagreement is not a reason to
      flag a contribution.
    </p>

    <div id="topicality">
      <p>
        <strong>Topicality:</strong>
        Submissions should materially concern how AI affects work, creativity,
        power, or human agency. This includes labor and organizations, tools and
        craft, education and research, governance and institutions, safety and
        accountability, ownership and access, and the social consequences of
        deploying these systems.
      </p>
      <p>
        Prefer primary sources, careful reporting, substantive analysis, and
        work that will remain useful after the current news cycle. Product
        announcements, outrage bait, context-free social posts, generic
        technology news, and claims that cannot be inspected are usually poor
        submissions.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p id="self-promo">
      <strong>Self-promotion:</strong>
      Authors and builders are encouraged to participate, disclose their
      connection, answer questions, and contribute beyond their own work.
      Self-promotion should be less than a quarter of a member's submissions
      and comments. Thneed is a discussion community, not a distribution
      channel.
    </p>

    <p id="brigading">
      <strong>No brigading:</strong>
      Do not use Thneed to direct an outrage mob, voting campaign, or harassment
      toward a person, project, issue tracker, or other community. For
      contentious subjects, submit a well-contextualized overview rather than
      dropping readers into the middle of a dispute.
    </p>

    <h2 id="submissions">Submissions and discussion</h2>

    <p>
      Use the original, descriptive title unless it is misleading or unclear.
      Do not add “Ask Thneed” to a title; submit a text story when you want to
      ask the community a question. Look for an existing discussion before
      submitting a link, and add context in a comment when the relevance is not
      obvious.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tagging">Tags</h2>

    <p>
      Every submission has one or more tags selected from the community's
      <%= link_to "tag list", tags_path %>. Tags keep the site coherent while
      allowing members to <%= link_to "filter", filters_path %> subjects they do
      not want to see. All stories are visible by default. Tag additions and
      changes are handled through the existing on-site administration and
      moderation process.
    </p>

    <h2 id="invitations">Invitations</h2>

    <p>
      Invitations slow spam and help new members learn the community's norms.
      The <%= link_to "invitation tree", users_tree_path %> is public, and
      profiles show who invited each member. Invite people you believe will
      contribute thoughtfully; invitations are a line of accountability, not a
      claim of status.
    </p>

    <p>
      The best way to join is to ask someone you recognize in the
      <%= link_to "invitation tree", users_tree_path %>.
      <% if Rails.application.allow_invitation_requests? %>
        If you do not know a member, you may
        <%= link_to "request an invitation", "/invitations/request" %>.
      <% else %>
        Invitation requests are currently closed.
      <% end %>
    </p>

    <p>
      Members are considered new for their first
      <%= User::NEW_USER_DAYS %> days. Some submission, flagging, suggestion,
      and invitation abilities are limited during that period while they learn
      the site's expectations.
    </p>

    <h2 id="ranking">Ranking</h2>

    <p>
      Story and comment ranking comes from member activity. Each member's vote
      has equal weight. Moderators cannot secretly raise or lower a submission;
      they participate with the same votes as everyone else. Public tag
      settings may adjust story hotness consistently for every submission with
      that tag.
    </p>

    <h2 id="flags">Flagging</h2>

    <p>
      Flags identify problems that need moderator attention, not opinions you
      dislike. Stories may be flagged as off-topic, duplicates, broken links,
      or spam. Comments may be flagged as off-topic, me-too, trolling, unkind,
      or spam. Flagging requires <%= User::MIN_KARMA_TO_FLAG %> karma, and
      repeated flags place an item in the moderation review queue.
    </p>

    <h2 id="moderation">Transparent moderation</h2>

    <p>
      Moderator actions are recorded in the public
      <%= link_to "moderation log", moderations_path %>, with the responsible
      moderator and reason. Banned profiles identify the ban and its reason.
      Thneed does not shadow-ban members. Difficult cases require judgment; the
      log makes those decisions visible and open to scrutiny.
    </p>

    <h2 id="source">Source and attribution</h2>

    <p>
      Thneed is a fork of
      <a href="https://github.com/lobsters/lobsters">Lobsters</a>, whose
      interaction model and open-source code made this community possible. The
      source remains available under the
      <a href="https://github.com/lobsters/lobsters/blob/main/LICENSE">3-clause BSD license</a>.
      We retain that attribution with gratitude.
    </p>
  </div>
</div>
